From 11b650d5a49c897ab2e2f62196ada7d247c0f0cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 03:36:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] application, VideoPlayer: a disc image behind an address that
 does not say so plays as a disc

Kodi takes a disc image by the extension of its address. A streaming add-on
gives the address of its resolver, often without the name of the file, and a
CDN may give the file an address without an extension and tell what it is by
its type (application/x-iso9660-image) or by the name in its
Content-Disposition. Such a Blu-ray image was opened as a video file and did
not play: measured in a VM (an image of this tree before), an add-on address
without extension that redirects to an .iso, and one whose CDN address has
no extension but the type of an image, played nothing in 180 s.

Now:
- CApplicationPlay, after the add-on resolved the item, asks an address of a
  web server once where it leads and what it is (CCurlFile::ProbeTarget(): a
  HEAD, or a GET that stops at its first data where HEAD is refused, as
  Stat() asks), when its extension says nothing of a video, sound or
  playlist and its type is not known (or is the generic one). A disc image
  plays as a disc from the address it leads to, with the add-on's options;
  the choices for a disc (its menu, the main title, the list of titles)
  apply as for any other image. An address that only its type or name says
  is an image is remembered as one (CHttpSourceMemory), and
  URIUtils::IsDiscImage() and CURL::IsDiscImage() take it so.
- The stream factory of the player takes where an address leads from what
  was asked a moment ago (by that probe, or by the HEAD that asks a stream
  for its type, CCurlFile::Stat(), which now remembers its redirect) instead
  of opening it again: one request less to the add-on's resolver at every
  start (measured at the start of a Matroska file: three requests to the
  resolver before, two now: the two HEADs that ask its type, as before).
- What the factory finds is a disc image (by the extension of where the
  address led, or its type) is opened as a disc, where nothing before found
  it.
One INFO line for each decision. Measured after, in the same VM (with the
volume of the image kept and read in pieces, the commits that follow): the
add-on address without extension played its main title in 2.6 to 2.9 s, the
CDN address told by its type in 2.3 to 2.5 s.
Credit: the idea of recognising an image
behind a redirect or by its type is mephis's (flhs0606/LibreELEC-Hades,
4fdd375477); written anew here, with one request instead of a probe of its
own, and without its other changes.
